<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry Sylvester Williams</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1869–<a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry Sylvester Williams was a Trinidadian lawyer, activist, councillor and writer, among the founders of the Pan-African movement. As a law student in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> he founded the African Association in 1897 with Alice Kinloch and Thomas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a> Thompson, and in July 1900 organised the first Pan-African Conference at Westminster Town Hall. He became the first black man called to the bar in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> in 1903 and in 1906 won a seat on Marylebone Borough Council, among the first people of African descent elected to public office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-sylvester-williams.jpg" alt="Henry Sylvester Williams" width="320" height="513" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ernest Herbert Mills · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S._Williams_1905.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
